# ledgerkit

> "Plain-text accounting (hledger-compatible)" — the CLI's own description
> string (current-state@v1#current-state-004).

ledgerkit is a Python implementation of the hledger plain-text accounting
tool.

## Status

As of the repository revision this document was drafted from, the
package's declared version is `1.0.0.dev1`, it targets Python `>=3.8`, and
it declares zero required runtime dependencies in `pyproject.toml`. An
optional `pandas` extra (`pandas>=1.3`) is available for callers who want
DataFrame-based access to report output (current-state@v1#current-state-001).

At that same revision, running the project's own test suite reportedly
produced 935 collected tests, 29 skipped, and zero failures or errors
(`OK (skipped=29)`). This specific figure is a point-in-time snapshot of
one test run rather than an independently re-confirmed, ongoing guarantee
(current-state@v1#current-state-003).

## Command-line interface

ledgerkit exposes exactly six report/validation subcommands through a
single flat `argparse` parser, selected via one required positional
`command` argument: `balance`, `register`, `accounts`, `print`, `stats`,
`check`. There is no `argparse` subparsers tree, so no subcommand has its
own distinct `--help` text — every subcommand's `--help` output is
byte-identical to the top-level `--help` output
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-001; current-state@v1#current-state-004,
current-state@v1#current-state-005).

A shared set of global flags applies uniformly to every command regardless
of which report is selected: `--version`; `-v`/`--verbose` (for `stats`,
also shows commodity names); `-1` (stats only: a single tab-separated
output line); `-o`/`--output-file FILE`; `-f`/`--file FILE` (repeatable);
`-s`/`--strict`; `-I`/`--ignore-assertions`; `-q`/`--query TERMS`; and
`-c`/`--commodity-style STYLE` (repeatable) (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-002).

### Journal file resolution

For every command except `check`, the journal file is resolved with a
strict four-tier precedence: one or more `-f`/`--file` flags, then a
single positional `journal` argument as shorthand, then the
`$LEDGER_FILE` environment variable, then `~/.hledger.journal` if
present. Combining `-f` with a positional argument is a hard error, and
exhausting all four sources without a result exits with "no journal file
specified." For the `check` command specifically, any positional
arguments are read as check *names*, never as a journal-file shorthand —
`check` must get its journal from `-f`, the environment variable, or the
default (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-003).

## Reports

### `balance`

`balance` computes per-commodity net balances for each account. The
underlying `reports.balance()` function returns a flat mapping (account →
commodity → net amount) by default, or, with `tree=True`, a list of rows
that additionally include every implicit parent account with aggregated
self-plus-descendant amounts and an `is_subtotal` marker
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-004).

The CLI prints one line per (account, commodity) pair — right-aligned
amount first, account name shown only on that account's last row — a
dashed separator sized to the widest amount/total string, then one
grand-total line per nonzero commodity (or a bare `"0"` if everything nets
to zero). Zero-net rows are omitted, except that a depth-0 `"..."` row
(produced by a `depth:0` query) is always printed regardless of its own
value, including when it is nonzero because the queried subset doesn't
itself balance to zero. Negative amounts are wrapped in ANSI red escape
codes (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-005).

### `register`

`register`'s underlying `reports.register()` function returns a
chronological list of rows whose `running_balance` is described as a
single cumulative sum across every row in output order, not re-grouped or
re-aggregated per account — even when a depth setting causes several
postings' displayed account names to collapse to the same string
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-006). That specific accumulation-under-depth-
clipping interaction has not been independently re-confirmed beyond the
original claim, so it is stated here with that caveat.

On screen, `register` prints one fixed-width line per row (date,
description, abbreviated account, right-aligned amount, right-aligned
running balance). The date and description columns are blanked on every
row after a transaction's first posting, long account names are
abbreviated by progressively shortening leading (non-last)
colon-separated components, and a running balance of exactly zero prints
as a bare `"0"` (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-007).

### `accounts`

`accounts` prints the sorted, deduplicated list of account names, one per
line, with no separator or total-line convention — a query that matches
nothing produces genuinely empty output, unlike `balance`
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-008).

### `print`

`print` reprints each matching transaction in journal-like text: a header
line with date, status flag, and description, followed by one indented
line per posting. A `-q`/`--query` filter on `print` matches whole
transactions — if any posting matches, every posting of that transaction
is printed, not just the matching one — and `print` deliberately ignores
`depth:` entirely, matching hledger's own `print` semantics
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-009).

`print` does not serialize inline comments: neither a transaction
header's nor a posting's inline comment is ever printed. This is a real,
confirmed divergence from the journal-text round-trip that
`ledgerkit.writer.transaction_to_text()` provides for the same data.
Whether this omission is a deliberate scope decision for `print` or an
unnoted gap is an open, unresolved question that the available evidence
does not settle (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-010).

### `stats`

`stats`'s underlying `reports.stats()` function returns a dataclass
holding only deterministic journal data (transaction counts, date range,
account/commodity counts, and so on); elapsed-time and
transactions-per-second figures are measured and formatted only by the
CLI layer, never stored on that dataclass. By default the CLI prints a
multi-line labeled report; with `-1` it instead prints one tab-separated
line (version, source file, elapsed time, transactions/second); with
`-v`/`--verbose`, the "Commodities" line additionally lists commodity
names (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-011).

`stats` is the one report function where a `depth:` query constraint
*excludes* accounts deeper than the limit from the account count, rather
than clipping/aggregating them the way `balance`, `register`, and
`accounts` all do — described as a deliberate, source-confirmed exception
rather than an oversight (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-012).

As a concrete illustration at one recorded point in time: running `stats`
against the project's own sample fixture produced a non-empty, multi-line
report (for example `Txns : 5 (0.2 per day)`, `Accounts : 6 (depth 3)`),
while running `check` against the same fixture succeeded with exit code 0
and produced no stdout output at all — a passing `check` run is silent by
design, with the exit code as the only success signal
(current-state@v1#current-state-008).

### `check`

`check` has a distinct control-flow path: an unconditional "basic checks"
gate runs before every command (including `check` itself), alongside
`check`'s own named-check logic. `-I`/`--ignore-assertions` suppresses
only the "assertions" basic check, in both that gate and in `check`'s own
explicit run; `-s`/`--strict` additionally runs a stricter check set for
every non-`check` command, and is forwarded into `check` itself
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-015).

### Report specs (Python API only)

`reports.balance_from_spec()` computes a structured, multi-section
balance report (for example, an income statement with an inverted
"Income" section) from `ReportSpec`/`ReportSection` dataclasses. It is
reachable from the Python API (`ledgerkit.balance_from_spec`,
`ledgerkit.ReportSpec`, `ledgerkit.ReportSection`) but, as of this
revision, is not wired to any of the six CLI subcommands
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-013).

## Journal text serialization

`ledgerkit.writer.transaction_to_text()` serializes a single transaction
to hledger journal-format text designed to round-trip through the parser
back to an equivalent transaction (same date, flag, description,
postings, amounts, and comments). Amount columns are right-aligned to a
shared column computed from the widest account name among postings that
carry an explicit amount, and an elided posting is printed as a bare
account name with no amount. `journal_to_text()` serializes a whole
journal's transactions separated by exactly one blank line each
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-014) — this part of the claim is the more
broadly corroborated half of it. As of this revision, `journal_to_text()`
is also stated to explicitly not serialize directives (`account`,
`commodity`, `payee`, `P`); callers needing full round-trip fidelity for
directives are expected to keep the raw source file instead — this
directive-exclusion detail is the more narrowly-evidenced half of the same
claim (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-014).

## Journal format support

### Transactions and dates

A transaction header line accepts dates in several forms — `YYYY-MM-DD`,
`YYYY/MM/DD`, `YYYY.MM.DD`, and year-omitted forms with optional leading
zeros and mixed separators — optionally followed by a secondary date
introduced with `=`, an optional status flag (`*` cleared, `!` pending),
an optional parenthesized code, a description, and an optional
`;`-introduced inline comment (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-001).

Dates elsewhere in a journal accept an optional 4-digit year followed by
a 1-2 digit month and day separated by `-`, `/`, or `.`; when the year is
omitted, a default year (itself overridable via the `Y` directive) is
substituted, and an invalid calendar date is rejected
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-002).

### Postings and amounts

A posting line is split into account and amount on the first run of two
or more whitespace characters, which lets an account name containing a
single internal space survive intact; a posting with no amount token
after that split is treated as an elided amount, to be resolved later
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-003).

Amount parsing supports prefix and suffix commodity symbols, a leading
minus sign or a minus sign appearing after a prefix symbol,
comma-thousands/period-decimal formatting by default (reversible via a
`decimal-mark` directive), E-notation, and space-separated digit groups
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-004).

`resolve_elision()` leaves a transaction's postings unchanged unless
exactly one posting has an elided amount; in that case it computes the
net of every explicit posting per commodity and replaces the elided
posting with one synthetic, `inferred=True` posting per commodity (sorted
by symbol), each the negation of that commodity's net. A transaction with
two or more elided postings is in practice rejected earlier, at parse
time, before `resolve_elision` ever sees it in that shape
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-005).

A posting amount may also carry a balance assertion (`=`, `==`, `=*`,
`==*`) and/or a cost annotation (` @ price` / ` @@ total`) and/or lot
annotations (`{amount}`, `{{amount}}`, `[date]`, `(label)`); these are
stated to be mutually composable on one posting line and are stripped and
parsed in a fixed order (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-009).

A posting's own comment may carry a `date`/`date2` tag that overrides
that posting's effective date; only the posting's own first occurrence of
such a tag takes effect, the transaction's own tags have no such override
effect, and an unparseable date value in the tag is a hard error
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-010).

### Comments

Comment syntax distinguishes four cases: a column-0 `;` or `#` line is
always a silently-skipped top-level comment, never attached to any
transaction; an indented `;`-led line inside an open transaction block is
appended to the last-seen posting's (or, if none yet, the transaction's)
inline comment; an indented `#`-led line inside a block extends the
transaction's source span without being attached to any comment field;
and a same-line comment after `;` on a header or posting line is captured
directly. Only `;`-led comments are scanned for tags — `#`-led comments
never are (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-006).

### Directives

The parser recognizes a substantial set of non-indented directives:
`account`, `commodity`, `payee`, `tag`, `decimal-mark`, `P` (market
price), `alias`/`end aliases`, `Y` (default year), `D` (default commodity
and style), `apply account`/`end apply account`, and `comment`/`end
comment` (block comment). It also recognizes, but does not implement the
bodies of, `~` (periodic transaction rule) and `=` (auto-posting rule)
headers, emitting a warning for each in lenient mode rather than treating
them as hard errors (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-007). This
inventory does not address what happens when one of these directives
appears in the middle of an already-open transaction block — that
interaction is outside what the available evidence for this inventory
examined.

### Strict vs. lenient parsing

The parser offers two public entry points with different error policies.
`parse_string` raises on the first malformed line. `parse_string_lenient`
is designed to never raise: for essentially every directive handler, it
instead collects each error/warning into a returned list, discards the
malformed transaction, and resumes parsing at the next blank line or
transaction header — intended for use cases such as editor integrations
that re-parse on every keystroke. As of this revision, a malformed
non-indented `commodity` directive (an empty body, an unterminated quote,
or an unparseable body) is a confirmed exception to that guarantee: it
raises unconditionally even under `parse_string_lenient`. This is the one
exception the available evidence names explicitly; it should not be read
as a guarantee that no other case can break the "never raises" contract
(journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-008).

## hledger compatibility tracking

ledgerkit tracks its behavioural compatibility with hledger through a
structured compatibility register (`dev-docs/compat-register/`), rather
than an informal list. Each entry is a YAML file classified under a
closed, five-value `kind` taxonomy — `compatible`, `extension`,
`intentional_divergence`, `unsupported`, `unexplained_mismatch` — each
mapped to a corresponding `relationship` value (compatible→equivalent,
extension→superset, unsupported→subset, intentional_divergence→
incomparable); `unexplained_mismatch` has no single fixed relationship
(compat-system@v1#compat-system-001).

Each entry also carries a four-tier `status`: `proposed`, `self-verified`
(confirmed by the same session that implemented the feature, with real
command output), `verified` (confirmed only by a separately-dispatched
differential-testing role — never written directly by the implementing
session), and `final`. This authorship restriction is a written policy
that was found to hold, without exception, across every entry carrying
one of these statuses at the time it was checked
(compat-system@v1#compat-system-002).

A dedicated resolution lifecycle governs `unexplained_mismatch` entries:
resolving one requires creating a brand-new entry (new id, kind, and
status) that references the old one, while the original mismatch entry
is kept unmodified except for an added back-reference — its own `kind`
field is never edited in place. As of the recorded survey, this mechanism
had been exercised exactly twice, and both of the register's
`unexplained_mismatch` entries had been resolved
(compat-system@v1#compat-system-005).

As of one recorded survey of the register, it held 49 entries: 40
classified `compatible`, 6 `unsupported`, 2 `unexplained_mismatch` (both
since resolved, per the lifecycle above), and 1 `intentional_divergence`,
with no entry yet using the `extension` kind. Of those 49, 26 were still
at `status: proposed` — not yet checked against the real hledger binary
by an independently-dispatched verifier — 9 were `self-verified`, and 14
were `verified`; no entry had yet reached `final`. These specific counts
describe a point-in-time snapshot of an actively-changing register, not a
fixed, permanent figure (compat-system@v1#compat-system-003,
compat-system@v1#compat-system-004).

## Known current limitations

A few behaviours are worth calling out explicitly rather than only
discovering them by use:

- `print` does not preserve inline comments on round-trip through the
  journal text, and whether this is intentional is an open, unresolved
  question (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-010).
- A malformed `commodity` directive is not actually caught leniently by
  `parse_string_lenient`, despite that function's documented "never
  raises" contract (journal-parsing@v1#journal-parsing-008).
- `check`'s positional arguments are read as check names, not as a
  journal-file shorthand, unlike every other command's positional
  argument (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-003).
- `reports.balance_from_spec()` and its `ReportSpec`/`ReportSection`
  dataclasses are usable from the Python API but are not wired to any
  CLI subcommand (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-013).
- Over half of the hledger-compatibility register's entries were, as of
  one recorded point, still at the lowest verification tier
  (`proposed`), meaning the corresponding compatibility claims had not
  yet been checked against the real hledger binary by an independent
  process (compat-system@v1#compat-system-004).
